@baseUrl = http://localhost:5128
@HostAddress = {{baseUrl}}
@companyAdminEmail = admin@al-salamah.com
@companyAdminPassword = User@123456
@missingJob = 3f9a1c22-7b44-4e18-9d05-6c2e8a5f1b70

### =====================================================================
### 13. ENGINEERING REVIEW & QUOTATION ENGINE (FR-ENG-01..06)
### =====================================================================
### The commercial half of a job: the consulting engineer's SBC 801 evaluation either clears it to
### pricing or hands the intake back to the client for correction, and the quotation engine then
### prices it — computing subtotal, segregated VAT and grand total server-side from the lines, and
### gating a thin-margin quote behind a written management clearance (FR-ENG-05).
###
### The full lifecycle — both engineering outcomes, the revision loop, margin-floor clearance, the
### client's accept/decline decision, and the client-vs-staff split of the same quotation — is
### covered exhaustively by EngineeringAndQuotationLifecycleTests (xUnit) and by a scripted live run
### over six real accounts against SQL Server. This suite is a smoke test of the surface with
### explicit assertions. Field casing is camelCase (D-8).

### 13.1 Company Admin login
# @name companyAdminLogin
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/v1/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json
Accept-Language: en

{ "email": "{{companyAdminEmail}}", "password": "{{companyAdminPassword}}" }

?? status == 200

@companyAdminToken = {{companyAdminLogin.data.token}}

### ---------------------------------------------------------------------
### Engineering review (FR-ENG-01/02)
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------

### 13.2 Even a CompanyAdmin cannot record an engineering verdict -> 403
### Engineering.Review is granted to ConsultingEngineer alone. Management owns triage and
### commercial authorization, not the SBC 801 judgement — a company owner cannot sign off their own
### building's compliance. Authorization runs ahead of validation and lookup, so this is 403 rather
### than the 404 the non-existent job would otherwise produce.
###
### The verdict paths themselves — compliant, non-compliant, mandatory remarks, hazard-class range
### — need an engineer's token and are covered by EngineeringAndQuotationLifecycleTests and the
### scripted live run.
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/v1/workflow-instances/{{missingJob}}/engineering-review
Authorization: Bearer {{companyAdminToken}}
Content-Type: application/json
Accept-Language: en

{
  "occupancyHazardClass": 3,
  "isolatedCompartmentAreaM2": 2300,
  "requiredSprinklerDensity": 0.2,
  "pumpHeadRatingMeters": 75,
  "smokeVentilationAirChangesPerHour": 6,
  "complianceStatus": 1,
  "technicalRemarks": "Compliant with SBC 801."
}

?? status == 403

### 13.3 The engineering trail for a job that does not exist -> 404
### Reading the trail is WorkflowInstances.View, which management does hold — so this reaches the
### handler and 404s on the job. An empty list here would be indistinguishable from a real job
### nobody has reviewed yet.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/v1/workflow-instances/{{missingJob}}/engineering-review
Authorization: Bearer {{companyAdminToken}}
Accept-Language: en

?? status == 404

### 13.4 Resubmitting a corrected intake that does not exist -> 404
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/v1/intake/{{missingJob}}/resubmit
Authorization: Bearer {{companyAdminToken}}
Accept-Language: en

?? status == 404

### ---------------------------------------------------------------------
### Quotation (FR-ENG-03/04/05)
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------

### 13.5 Pricing a job that does not exist -> 404
PUT {{HostAddress}}/api/v1/workflow-instances/{{missingJob}}/quotation
Authorization: Bearer {{companyAdminToken}}
Content-Type: application/json
Accept-Language: en

{
  "lines": [
    { "description": "FM-200 cylinders", "unitOfMeasurement": "each", "quantity": 4, "unitRate": 3250, "unitCost": 2100 }
  ]
}

?? status == 404

### 13.6 A quotation with no priced lines -> 400
### An empty quotation is not a zero-value offer, it is an unfinished document.
PUT {{HostAddress}}/api/v1/workflow-instances/{{missingJob}}/quotation
Authorization: Bearer {{companyAdminToken}}
Content-Type: application/json
Accept-Language: en

{ "lines": [] }

?? status == 400

### 13.7 A negative quantity -> 400
PUT {{HostAddress}}/api/v1/workflow-instances/{{missingJob}}/quotation
Authorization: Bearer {{companyAdminToken}}
Content-Type: application/json
Accept-Language: en

{
  "lines": [
    { "description": "Detector loop", "unitOfMeasurement": "m", "quantity": -5, "unitRate": 18.75, "unitCost": 11.4 }
  ]
}

?? status == 400

### 13.8 Reading the staff quotation of a job that does not exist -> 404
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/v1/workflow-instances/{{missingJob}}/quotation
Authorization: Bearer {{companyAdminToken}}
Accept-Language: en

?? status == 404

### 13.9 Authorizing a quotation that does not exist -> 404
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/v1/workflow-instances/{{missingJob}}/quotation/authorize
Authorization: Bearer {{companyAdminToken}}
Content-Type: application/json
Accept-Language: en

{}

?? status == 404

### ---------------------------------------------------------------------
### The client side of a quotation (FR-ENG-06)
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------

### 13.10 CompanyAdmin is not a Client, so the client quotation view refuses them -> 403
### Quotations.Prepare and Quotations.Authorize do not imply ClientRequests.View: management reads a
### quotation through the staff endpoint, which carries cost and margin. The client projection is a
### different document for a different audience, not a permission tier.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/v1/my-requests/{{missingJob}}/quotation
Authorization: Bearer {{companyAdminToken}}
Accept-Language: en

?? status == 403

### 13.11 Likewise for recording a client's decision -> 403
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/v1/my-requests/{{missingJob}}/quotation/respond
Authorization: Bearer {{companyAdminToken}}
Content-Type: application/json
Accept-Language: en

{ "accept": true }

?? status == 403

### ---------------------------------------------------------------------
### Authentication
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------

### 13.12 Unauthenticated engineering review -> 401
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/v1/workflow-instances/{{missingJob}}/engineering-review
Content-Type: application/json
Accept-Language: en

{ "occupancyHazardClass": 3, "isolatedCompartmentAreaM2": 100, "requiredSprinklerDensity": 0.2, "complianceStatus": 1 }

?? status == 401

### 13.13 Unauthenticated pricing -> 401
PUT {{HostAddress}}/api/v1/workflow-instances/{{missingJob}}/quotation
Content-Type: application/json
Accept-Language: en

{ "lines": [] }

?? status == 401

### ---------------------------------------------------------------------
### Engineering attachments (FR-ENG-01)
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------
### The engineer's own output — a stamped drawing, a hydraulic calculation sheet, a deficiency
### report — as opposed to the client's blueprints, which arrive at intake. Until this shipped an
### engineer could record an SBC 801 verdict and could not attach the calculation justifying it.
###
### The ordering underneath is the interesting part and is covered by EngineeringAttachmentTests
### (xUnit): evidence is uploaded while the job is UnderReview and is unclaimed, and recording the
### verdict stamps every unclaimed attachment on that job with the review it just created. A
### re-review after a revision cycle sweeps up only its own round, because the previous round's
### attachments already carry the previous verdict and the entity refuses to reassign them.

### 13.14 Unauthenticated upload -> 401
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/v1/workflow-instances/{{missingJob}}/engineering-review/attachments
Content-Type: multipart/form-data; boundary=AmanixEngBoundary

--AmanixEngBoundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="file"; filename="calc.pdf"
Content-Type: application/pdf

%PDF-1.4
%%EOF
--AmanixEngBoundary--

?? status == 401

### 13.15 Unauthenticated listing -> 401
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/v1/workflow-instances/{{missingJob}}/engineering-review/attachments

?? status == 401

### 13.16 Even a CompanyAdmin cannot attach engineering evidence -> 403
### The same segregation of duties as 13.2, extended to the evidence. Engineering.Review belongs
### to the ConsultingEngineer alone: management owns triage and pricing, the engineer owns the
### verdict — and the calculation behind a verdict is part of the verdict, not a general document
### any manager may file against the job.
###
### Asserted with a job id that does not exist, and the answer is still 403 rather than 404. That
### ordering is the second guarantee here: the permission is checked before the job is resolved,
### so a caller without it cannot use this endpoint to discover which job ids are real.
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/v1/workflow-instances/{{missingJob}}/engineering-review/attachments
Authorization: Bearer {{companyAdminToken}}
Accept-Language: en
Content-Type: multipart/form-data; boundary=AmanixEngBoundary

--AmanixEngBoundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="file"; filename="calc.pdf"
Content-Type: application/pdf

%PDF-1.4
%%EOF
--AmanixEngBoundary--

?? status == 403

### 13.17 Listing the evidence needs only WorkflowInstances.View -> 404 for a missing job
### Reading is not recording. Everyone who works a job can see what the engineer attached —
### management, operations, sales — because the drawing and the calculation are what the next
### stage prices and builds from. Only writing is restricted.
###
### 404 rather than 403 proves CompanyAdmin genuinely holds this one: the request got past the
### permission filter and failed on the job id.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/v1/workflow-instances/{{missingJob}}/engineering-review/attachments
Authorization: Bearer {{companyAdminToken}}
Accept-Language: en

?? status == 404

### 13.18 The permission gate runs before the request body is read -> 403
### No file part at all, which would otherwise be a 400 binding failure. The 403 proves the
### unauthorized caller's upload was never parsed — a 400 here would mean the server processed a
### multipart body on behalf of someone with no right to submit one.
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/v1/workflow-instances/{{missingJob}}/engineering-review/attachments
Authorization: Bearer {{companyAdminToken}}
Accept-Language: en
Content-Type: multipart/form-data; boundary=AmanixEngBoundary

--AmanixEngBoundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="description"

a note with no file
--AmanixEngBoundary--

?? status == 403

### 13.19 And before the bytes are examined -> 403
### "MZ" is the two-byte signature every Windows executable starts with, renamed to .pdf. Uploads
### are typed by sniffing the leading bytes rather than by the extension or the declared content
### type — the classic upload vulnerability, closed by IntakeFileRules and proven by
### IntakeFileRulesTests (xUnit) and over the wire by suite 12.
###
### What this asserts is that the check is never reached for a caller who may not upload at all.
### Authorization first, then the job, then the bytes.
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/v1/workflow-instances/{{missingJob}}/engineering-review/attachments
Authorization: Bearer {{companyAdminToken}}
Accept-Language: en
Content-Type: multipart/form-data; boundary=AmanixEngBoundary

--AmanixEngBoundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="file"; filename="drawing.pdf"
Content-Type: application/pdf

MZ
--AmanixEngBoundary--

?? status == 403

### ---------------------------------------------------------------------
### The other side of the split: the engineer
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------
### Everything above asserts who is refused. These assert who is granted, which is the half that
### was missing until the demo seeder gained a predictable account per role. Before that only
### admin@ and deputy@ had fixed addresses and the other seven roles got Bogus-generated ones, so
### no suite could sign in as the person who actually does the work — and the coverage for those
### roles quietly consisted of 403 assertions alone.

### 13.20 Consulting Engineer login
# @name engineerLogin
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/v1/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json
Accept-Language: en

{ "email": "engineer@al-salamah.com", "password": "User@123456" }

?? status == 200

@engineerToken = {{engineerLogin.data.token}}

### 13.21 The engineer holds Engineering.Review -> 404, not 403
### The complement of 13.16, and the assertion that gives that one its meaning. A 403 on both
### sides would prove only that the endpoint is hard to reach; the pair proves the permission is
### split where it was designed to be split.
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/v1/workflow-instances/{{missingJob}}/engineering-review/attachments
Authorization: Bearer {{engineerToken}}
Accept-Language: en
Content-Type: multipart/form-data; boundary=AmanixEngBoundary

--AmanixEngBoundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="file"; filename="calc.pdf"
Content-Type: application/pdf

%PDF-1.4
%%EOF
--AmanixEngBoundary--

?? status == 404

### 13.22 The engineer may record a verdict too -> 404, not 403
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/v1/workflow-instances/{{missingJob}}/engineering-review
Authorization: Bearer {{engineerToken}}
Content-Type: application/json
Accept-Language: en

{ "occupancyHazardClass": 3, "isolatedCompartmentAreaM2": 100, "requiredSprinklerDensity": 0.2, "complianceStatus": 1 }

?? status == 404

### 13.23 An engineer may read the evidence -> 404 for a missing job
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/v1/workflow-instances/{{missingJob}}/engineering-review/attachments
Authorization: Bearer {{engineerToken}}
Accept-Language: en

?? status == 404

### 13.24 The engineer may PRICE the job -> 400, not 403
### Quotations.Prepare is held by the ConsultingEngineer and the SalesRepresentative, which is
### deliberate and easy to get backwards: the person who knows the technical scope is the person
### who can cost it. Pricing is not the privileged act here.
###
### The body is deliberately invalid — a quotation with no lines — and 400 is what proves the
### point. Validation runs after authorization and before the job is looked up, so reaching a
### validation error means the engineer passed the permission filter. A 403 would have come first
### and the request would never have been read. Asserting the permission through the error that
### comes *after* it needs no knowledge of the line schema, so this stays true when that schema
### changes.
PUT {{HostAddress}}/api/v1/workflow-instances/{{missingJob}}/quotation
Authorization: Bearer {{engineerToken}}
Content-Type: application/json
Accept-Language: en

{ "lines": [] }

?? status == 400
?? body isSuccess == false

### 13.25 ...but may not ISSUE it to the client -> 403
### This is where the split actually falls. Quotations.Authorize belongs to CompanyAdmin and
### DeputyAdmin alone (BR-05, equal authority), because issuing is the commitment: it is what
### clears a thin-margin quote against the floor and puts a number in front of the client. The
### engineer prepares, management commits.
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/v1/workflow-instances/{{missingJob}}/quotation/authorize
Authorization: Bearer {{engineerToken}}
Content-Type: application/json
Accept-Language: en

{ "belowFloorJustification": null }

?? status == 403

### 13.26 An engineer is not a client -> 403 on the client portal
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/v1/my-requests
Authorization: Bearer {{engineerToken}}
Accept-Language: en

?? status == 403
